      <div class="amp-stack">
        <section class="amp-section">
          <h2>First things first: you belong here</h2>
          <p>
            I want every student who walks into my classroom to know two things right away:
            <strong>you matter</strong>, and <strong>you belong here</strong>. School should feel safe —
            safe enough to ask a question, safe enough to get something wrong, and safe enough to try
            something new without worrying that everyone is judging you.
          </p>
          <p>
            I don't believe students show up as empty containers waiting to be filled. You already bring
            experiences, languages, cultures, jokes, games, stories, and ideas that can make our class
            better. My job isn't to gatekeep knowledge. It's to put you in situations where you get as
            many chances to learn as possible — from me, from your classmates, and from the work itself.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="amp-section">
          <h2>What I believe about learning</h2>
          <ul class="amp-bullets">
            <li><strong>Learning is something you do, not something I do to you.</strong> I guide. You build. We figure things out together.</li>
            <li><strong>The process matters more than a perfect first try.</strong> Drafting, testing, revising, and trying again are signs of real learning — not failure.</li>
            <li><strong>Mistakes are data.</strong> When something doesn't work, that's useful information. We use it to get better.</li>
            <li><strong>Hard is good — when it's the right kind of hard.</strong> I try to give you challenges that stretch you without crushing you.</li>
            <li><strong>Sometimes another student is the best teacher in the room.</strong> If a classmate explains something in a way that clicks for you, that's a win.</li>
            <li><strong>Words matter.</strong> I try to use language that includes your reality — your family, your culture, your experience.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="amp-section">
          <h2>What my classroom is like</h2>
          <p>
            Technology class is hands-on. We code. We design games and projects. We make videos. We solve
            real problems — like figuring out how a computer "thinks," turning an idea into a working
            prototype, or building something you can actually show off.
          </p>
          <p>
            I like class to be active, not passive. That means you'll be doing things — writing
            instructions, debugging code, collaborating in groups, presenting work, and sometimes
            teaching me something I didn't know yet.
          </p>
          <p>
            We have routines that help us listen to each other. You've probably seen the
            <strong>talking ball</strong> (it's really a soft 20-sided dice). Simple rule: whoever has
            the ball is talking, and everyone else is listening — including me.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you've finished the main work for the day, you get <strong>Free Choice time</strong>.
            That name is intentional. You get freedom <em>and</em> choice — read, play a game, catch up
            on another class, or keep exploring the tech we were working on. It's not chaos. It's trust.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="amp-section">
          <h2>How I explain hard things</h2>
          <p>
            One of my favorite parts of teaching is taking a complicated idea and making it make sense.
            A mentor once told me that a great teacher can explain difficult concepts in a simple, clear way.
            I work hard at that.
          </p>
          <p>
            Here's a real example from my class: we were watching a video about binary code, and the
            narrator said <em>"abstraction is the foundation of all computing."</em> I realized a lot of
            students didn't know what "abstraction" meant — and without that word, the whole video was
            just noise.
          </p>
          <p>
            So I drew a smiley face on the board and asked, "What is this?" You said smiley face. Happy.
            Face. Person. A circle with two dots and a curve. <em>All correct.</em> Then I explained:
            abstraction is when we strip away the details that don't matter and keep the core idea. A
            stick figure is an abstract person. At their core, computers are just 1s and 0s — on or off.
          </p>
          <p>
            That's how I want to teach: meet you where you are, build a bridge to where we're going, and
            make sure the vocabulary actually means something to <em>you</em>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="amp-section">
          <h2>Don't be a Gort</h2>
          <p>
            One of the most important lessons I ever learned in school came from a teacher who warned us
            never to be a <strong>Gort</strong> — someone who believes "that which is so, is so," and
            never questions anything. The opposite is better: <em>that which is so is not always so.</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            Things are the way they are because people made choices. Rules can change. Systems can
            improve. I want you to think critically, ask why, and form your own conclusions — not just
            repeat what you're told. In my class, I care about your reasoning at least as much as your
            final answer.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="amp-section">
          <h2>My promises to you</h2>
          <div class="amp-promise-grid">
            <div class="amp-promise">
              <strong>I will see the best in you</strong>
              <span>And I'll try to help you see it too — even on days when school feels hard.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="amp-promise">
              <strong>I will take you seriously</strong>
              <span>Your questions, your culture, your effort, and your ideas all count.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="amp-promise">
              <strong>I will be fair</strong>
              <span>Everyone deserves a real chance to learn — no matter how you learn best.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="amp-promise">
              <strong>I will be honest with you</strong>
              <span>If something isn't working, we'll talk about it and figure out a path forward.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="amp-promise">
              <strong>I will share the joy</strong>
              <span>I love learning new things, and I hope you feel that energy in our room.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="amp-promise">
              <strong>I will keep growing too</strong>
              <span>I'm still learning how to be a better teacher. Your feedback matters.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="amp-section">
          <h2>A little about me (the human stuff)</h2>
          <p>
            Before I was a classroom teacher, I worked as a paraprofessional in a special education
            program, then as a substitute teacher. Those experiences taught me that every student
            learns differently — and that caring about students isn't optional. It's the whole job.
          </p>
          <p>
            I run the after-school <strong>Dungeons &amp; Dragons</strong> club. I love games, stories,
            problem-solving, and anything that makes you think creatively.
          </p>
          {% include "about-mr-phil/gallery.njk" %}
          <p class="amp-quote amp-quote--block">
            "I refuse to be another brick in the wall. I will be dynamite to break down barriers — between
            ignorance and knowledge, and between 'us vs. them.' I want every student to see me as a
            participant in their joy."
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="amp-section">
          <h2>The little flowers</h2>
          <p>I wrote a short poem once that captures how I think about teaching middle schoolers:</p>
          <p class="amp-quote amp-quote--block">
            Wound not the little flowers when they just begin to bloom. Delicate and fragile, a tiny stick
            in the muddy ground — but how they stretch and flex and reach towards the Light.
          </p>
          <p>
            You're growing. Some days feel muddy. Some days feel bright. Either way, I'm here to support
            the stretch — not trample it. Show up, take a risk, ask for help when you need it, and let's
            build something worth being proud of.
          </p>
          <p class="amp-sig amp-sig--left">Welcome to Tech. I'm glad you're here. — Mr. Phil</p>
        </section>
